{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015JEfdKEhQm9jBkQ5MJGmWx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Report: Batch Stacked Reduction Round 0 Descriptors\n2\t\n3\t## Description\n4\t\n5\tBatch the per-trace stacked reduction Round 0 kernel launches (sumcheck block sums + PLE fold) using descriptor arrays. At APC 300, the stacked reduction Round 0 loop calls `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` once per backing trace matrix (~400 per segment), many of which are tiny (1-2 columns, height 2^5-2^8). Grouping same-height matrices into single batched kernel launches was expected to reduce ~800 kernel launches per segment to ~100, saving kernel launch overhead and improving SM utilization for the numerous tiny launches.\n6\t\n7\t## Implementation\n8\t\n9\t### CUDA kernels (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`)\n10\t- Added `StackedR0Desc` struct: `{trace_ptr, lambda_pows, trace_width}` for batched Round 0.\n11\t- Added `FoldPleDesc` struct: `{src, dst, trace_width}` for batched PLE fold.\n12\t- Added `upper_bound_search()` device function for binary search on column prefix sums.\n13\t- Added `batched_stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`: 2D grid (blocks_per_row, total_columns). Each block uses binary search on `col_prefix_sums` to find its descriptor and local column.\n14\t- Added `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`: Same column-based binary search approach.\n15\t- Added `_batched_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` launcher: launches batched block_sum kernel, then per-descriptor `final_reduce_block_sums<true>`.\n16\t- Added `_batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple` launcher.\n17\t\n18\t### FFI bindings (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`)\n19\t- Added `StackedR0Desc` and `FoldPleDesc` repr(C) structs with Send impls.\n20\t- Added extern \"C\" declarations for both batched launchers.\n21\t- Added safe Rust wrappers: `batched_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0`, `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple`.\n22\t\n23\t### Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`)\n24\t- **Round 0** (`batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`): Replaced per-trace loop with height-grouped batching. Traces are grouped by height, merged, and groups with >=10 traces use the batched kernel path. Small groups use the existing per-trace path.\n25\t- **PLE fold** (`fold_ple_evals`): Within each per-commit outer loop, traces are grouped by height and large groups (>=10) use the batched kernel.\n26\t- Added `BATCH_THRESHOLD = 10` module-level constant.\n27\t- Pre-computes max `block_sums` size upfront to avoid per-trace resize.\n28\t\n29\tNo deviations from the plan.\n30\t\n31\t## Results\n32\t\n33\t| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n34\t|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n35\t| **APC 300** | | | | | |\n36\t| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1127ms | 1122ms | -1333ms, 2.19x lower | -5ms, 1.00x |\n37\t| Stacked Reduction | 311ms | 74ms | 74ms | -237ms, 4.20x lower | 0ms, 1.00x |\n38\t| Round 0 | 662ms | 182ms | 188ms | -474ms, 3.52x lower | +6ms, 1.03x higher |\n39\t| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 379ms | 368ms | -422ms, 2.15x lower | -11ms, 1.03x lower |\n40\t| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 161ms | 162ms | -18ms, 1.11x lower | +1ms, 1.01x higher |\n41\t| Trace Commit | 406ms | 197ms | 197ms | -209ms, 2.06x lower | 0ms, 1.00x |\n42\t| WHIR | 100ms | 127ms | 128ms | +28ms, 1.28x higher | +1ms, 1.01x |\n43\t| **APC 100** | | | | | |\n44\t| STARK excl trace | — | 1302ms | 1301ms | — | -1ms, 1.00x |\n45\t| Round 0 | — | 165ms | 162ms | — | -3ms, 1.02x lower |\n46\t| Stacked Reduction | — | 70ms | 70ms | — | 0ms, 1.00x |\n47\t| **APC 0** | | | | | |\n48\t| STARK excl trace | — | 1793ms | 1796ms | — | +3ms, 1.00x |\n49\t| Round 0 | — | 179ms | 176ms | — | -3ms, 1.02x lower |\n50\t| Stacked Reduction | — | 77ms | 77ms | — | 0ms, 1.00x |\n51\t\n52\tAll changes are within measurement noise (±10ms).\n53\t\n54\t## Assessment\n55\t\n56\tThe optimization did **not** improve performance. All metrics are within measurement noise at all APC configurations. No regression at APC 0.\n57\t\n58\tThe expected 7ms improvement from eliminating ~1400 kernel launches was not realized. The per-launch CUDA overhead is ~2-3µs (confirmed by the previous `batch-fold-ple-descriptor-array` task), giving a theoretical savings of ~3-4ms — which is well below the measurement noise floor of ±10ms.\n59\t\n60\tAdditionally, the batched path introduces its own overhead:\n61\t- CPU-side descriptor array construction and sorting (~0.1ms)\n62\t- H2D upload of descriptor and prefix sum arrays (~0.1ms)  \n63\t- Binary search in the kernel for each block (~negligible GPU time)\n64\t\n65\tThe net savings after subtracting batching overhead is estimated at ~2-3ms, which is undetectable.\n66\t\n67\tThe stacked reduction Round 0 has already been optimized to 74ms at APC 300 (down from 311ms baseline), and the remaining time is dominated by GPU kernel execution (not launch overhead). The Stacked Reduction is now only 6.6% of STARK excl trace — too small a target for kernel launch batching to have a measurable impact.\n68\t\n69\t## Future Work\n70\t\n71\t- **Stacked Reduction is at diminishing returns**: At 74ms (6.6% of STARK excl trace at APC 300), further optimizations to stacked reduction have negligible impact on the overall metric.\n72\t- **Kernel launch overhead is not a bottleneck**: The CUDA driver pipeline already hides most inter-kernel launch gaps for kernels that execute on the default stream sequentially. Batching only removes gaps that were already hidden.\n73\t- **Focus should shift to remaining large components**: LogUp GKR (368ms, 33%), Round 0 (188ms, 17%), MLE Rounds (162ms, 14%), and Trace Commit (197ms, 18%) are the dominant costs at APC 300.\n74\t- **Kernel fusion** (combining multiple small sequential kernels into one) could potentially help more than kernel batching for the stacked reduction, since it would reduce actual GPU scheduling overhead rather than just CUDA API overhead.\n75\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6755d07-23c8-46a3-81f0-36e3b63f2ed6","uuid":"e0629e62-5e2f-4032-a116-4095d834b3c4","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:52:58.828Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-16-1600-batch-stacked-reduction-round0-descriptors/report.md","content":"# Report: Batch Stacked Reduction Round 0 Descriptors\n\n## Description\n\nBatch the per-trace stacked reduction Round 0 kernel launches (sumcheck block sums + PLE fold) using descriptor arrays. At APC 300, the stacked reduction Round 0 loop calls `stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` once per backing trace matrix (~400 per segment), many of which are tiny (1-2 columns, height 2^5-2^8). Grouping same-height matrices into single batched kernel launches was expected to reduce ~800 kernel launches per segment to ~100, saving kernel launch overhead and improving SM utilization for the numerous tiny launches.\n\n## Implementation\n\n### CUDA kernels (`crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/stacked_reduction.cu`)\n- Added `StackedR0Desc` struct: `{trace_ptr, lambda_pows, trace_width}` for batched Round 0.\n- Added `FoldPleDesc` struct: `{src, dst, trace_width}` for batched PLE fold.\n- Added `upper_bound_search()` device function for binary search on column prefix sums.\n- Added `batched_stacked_reduction_round0_block_sum_kernel`: 2D grid (blocks_per_row, total_columns). Each block uses binary search on `col_prefix_sums` to find its descriptor and local column.\n- Added `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel`: Same column-based binary search approach.\n- Added `_batched_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` launcher: launches batched block_sum kernel, then per-descriptor `final_reduce_block_sums<true>`.\n- Added `_batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple` launcher.\n\n### FFI bindings (`crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs`)\n- Added `StackedR0Desc` and `FoldPleDesc` repr(C) structs with Send impls.\n- Added extern \"C\" declarations for both batched launchers.\n- Added safe Rust wrappers: `batched_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0`, `batched_stacked_reduction_fold_ple`.\n\n### Rust orchestration (`crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`)\n- **Round 0** (`batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly`): Replaced per-trace loop with height-grouped batching. Traces are grouped by height, merged, and groups with >=10 traces use the batched kernel path. Small groups use the existing per-trace path.\n- **PLE fold** (`fold_ple_evals`): Within each per-commit outer loop, traces are grouped by height and large groups (>=10) use the batched kernel.\n- Added `BATCH_THRESHOLD = 10` module-level constant.\n- Pre-computes max `block_sums` size upfront to avoid per-trace resize.\n\nNo deviations from the plan.\n\n## Results\n\n| Metric | Baseline | Before Task | After Task | vs Baseline | vs Before |\n|--------|----------|-------------|------------|-------------|-----------|\n| **APC 300** | | | | | |\n| STARK excl trace | 2455ms | 1127ms | 1122ms | -1333ms, 2.19x lower | -5ms, 1.00x |\n| Stacked Reduction | 311ms | 74ms | 74ms | -237ms, 4.20x lower | 0ms, 1.00x |\n| Round 0 | 662ms | 182ms | 188ms | -474ms, 3.52x lower | +6ms, 1.03x higher |\n| LogUp GKR | 790ms | 379ms | 368ms | -422ms, 2.15x lower | -11ms, 1.03x lower |\n| MLE Rounds | 180ms | 161ms | 162ms | -18ms, 1.11x lower | +1ms, 1.01x higher |\n| Trace Commit | 406ms | 197ms | 197ms | -209ms, 2.06x lower | 0ms, 1.00x |\n| WHIR | 100ms | 127ms | 128ms | +28ms, 1.28x higher | +1ms, 1.01x |\n| **APC 100** | | | | | |\n| STARK excl trace | — | 1302ms | 1301ms | — | -1ms, 1.00x |\n| Round 0 | — | 165ms | 162ms | — | -3ms, 1.02x lower |\n| Stacked Reduction | — | 70ms | 70ms | — | 0ms, 1.00x |\n| **APC 0** | | | | | |\n| STARK excl trace | — | 1793ms | 1796ms | — | +3ms, 1.00x |\n| Round 0 | — | 179ms | 176ms | — | -3ms, 1.02x lower |\n| Stacked Reduction | — | 77ms | 77ms | — | 0ms, 1.00x |\n\nAll changes are within measurement noise (±10ms).\n\n## Assessment\n\nThe optimization did **not** improve performance. All metrics are within measurement noise at all APC configurations. No regression at APC 0.\n\nThe expected 7ms improvement from eliminating ~1400 kernel launches was not realized. The per-launch CUDA overhead is ~2-3µs (confirmed by the previous `batch-fold-ple-descriptor-array` task), giving a theoretical savings of ~3-4ms — which is well below the measurement noise floor of ±10ms.\n\nAdditionally, the batched path introduces its own overhead:\n- CPU-side descriptor array construction and sorting (~0.1ms)\n- H2D upload of descriptor and prefix sum arrays (~0.1ms)  \n- Binary search in the kernel for each block (~negligible GPU time)\n\nThe net savings after subtracting batching overhead is estimated at ~2-3ms, which is undetectable.\n\nThe stacked reduction Round 0 has already been optimized to 74ms at APC 300 (down from 311ms baseline), and the remaining time is dominated by GPU kernel execution (not launch overhead). The Stacked Reduction is now only 6.6% of STARK excl trace — too small a target for kernel launch batching to have a measurable impact.\n\n## Future Work\n\n- **Stacked Reduction is at diminishing returns**: At 74ms (6.6% of STARK excl trace at APC 300), further optimizations to stacked reduction have negligible impact on the overall metric.\n- **Kernel launch overhead is not a bottleneck**: The CUDA driver pipeline already hides most inter-kernel launch gaps for kernels that execute on the default stream sequentially. Batching only removes gaps that were already hidden.\n- **Focus should shift to remaining large components**: LogUp GKR (368ms, 33%), Round 0 (188ms, 17%), MLE Rounds (162ms, 14%), and Trace Commit (197ms, 18%) are the dominant costs at APC 300.\n- **Kernel fusion** (combining multiple small sequential kernels into one) could potentially help more than kernel batching for the stacked reduction, since it would reduce actual GPU scheduling overhead rather than just CUDA API overhead.\n","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":75}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EjpdeEr2LimiQAmwujQ1Ja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of the optimization history. Let me understand the current state:\n\n**Baseline (APC 300): STARK excl trace = 2455ms**\n**Current best (APC 300): STARK excl trace ≈ 1110-1127ms (2.19-2.21x lower vs baseline)**\n\nThe latest report shows the current breakdown at APC 300:\n- LogUp GKR: 368ms (33%)\n- Trace Commit: 197ms (18%)\n\n\n- Round 0: 188ms (17%)\n- MLE Rounds: 162ms (14%)\n- WHIR: 128ms (11.4%)\n- Stacked Reduction: 74ms (6.6%)\n\nLooking at the optimization history, I can see that CUDA API overhead has hit diminishing returns after extensive work, multi-stream parallelism helps with Round 0 and GKR input evaluation, VPMM pool state keeps causing regressions in GKR performance when allocation patterns change, and batching attempts for Round 0 haven't panned out.\n\nThe MLE rounds are bottlenecked by kernel execution, stacked reduction is nearly optimized out at 6.6%, and the L2 cache at 72MB is crucial for GKR kernel performance—reusing intermediate buffers across sequential AIR execution keeps data in cache. The biggest remaining opportunity is LogUp GKR at 368ms (33% of total), where I've already tried multi-stream parallelism and pre-allocated buffers successfully, but batch scatter, batch global, grid sizing, warp-tiled intermediates, and GPU transcript approaches all failed—the remaining time appears to be pure kernel execution on memory-bandwidth-limited operations.\n\nCommit is 197ms (18%), with batched scatter kernels bringing it down from 406ms but NTT and scatter still dominating. Round 0 sits at 188ms (17%) and is now kernel-execution-bound after successful multi-streaming and GPU poly extraction. MLE rounds are 162ms (14%) and also kernel-execution-bound despite batching and buffer optimizations. WHIR has actually regressed to 128ms (11.4%) from the baseline 100ms, and VPMM page size tuning didn't help.\n\nNow I need to run the current measurements and check the GPU availability before benchmarking. Let me review the recent reports more carefully to understand the current state better, especially the performance breakdowns, then measure where things stand right now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3357,"cache_read_input_tokens":41790,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3357},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6755d07-23c8-46a3-81f0-36e3b63f2ed6","uuid":"a8a8ad3b-fa4e-4a25-b001-fde7fd376d2b"}
