{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DDNv3HzSMALX5nuknPq5SN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017jPFMRdQeMataNDftVMvKn","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research MLE rounds scaling","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I'm optimizing a GPU STARK prover. MLE Rounds take 161ms at APC 300 (623 AIRs, 811M cells) vs 117ms at APC 0 (99 AIRs, 1904M cells) — so more AIRs with fewer cells takes MORE time.\n\nI need to understand what causes the per-trace/per-AIR overhead in MLE rounds. Search for:\n\n1. The MLE sumcheck round evaluation code in crates/cuda-backend/src/ — look for functions like `sumcheck_round`, `mle_round`, `fold_pingpong`, `batch_sumcheck_poly_eval`, etc.\n2. How per-trace work is structured — what happens for each trace in each MLE round?\n3. The `precompute_logup_combinations_kernel` which is called 791 times per proof — what triggers each call and what does it compute?\n4. The batched MLE kernels (`zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel`, `logup_batch_mle_kernel`) — how are traces grouped?\n\nFocus on crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck.rs or similar files. Report file paths, line numbers, and the per-round loop structure."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7453,"cache_read_input_tokens":51887,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7453},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6755d07-23c8-46a3-81f0-36e3b63f2ed6","uuid":"2e1cd4a1-a37f-436e-a4bd-4cf3e6af1a75"}
