{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ELPzSNHyPXcC9ovaVMG2YG","type":"tool_result","content":"3://! Many logup/zerocheck GPU helpers allocate temporary device buffers and launch kernels. Those\n12:    collections::hash_map::Entry,\n28:    calculate_n_logup,\n45:use rustc_hash::FxHashMap;\n60:    hash_scheme::GpuHashScheme,\n100:/// More threads = more concurrent kernels but more peak GPU memory and more\n105:/// instead of the monomial kernel for high num_y traces.\n106:/// This ratio can be tuned. Currently it is set to prefer the monomial kernel except for the\n308:    // GPU-side polynomial extraction: launch a small kernel to compute\n326:    // sum_buffer dropped here; cudaFreeAsync is ordered after the kernel on the same stream\n430:    let n_logup = calculate_n_logup(l_skip, total_interactions);\n501:    // not include the kernel times for the actual folding\n946:    // Cannot do it for logup because we need to calculate the sum claims.\n1151:        // All polynomial coefficients are written here by GPU extraction kernels,\n1232:        // kernel execution. With few large AIRs (e.g., APC 0 has ~20 per segment), each\n1233:        // kernel already saturates the GPU and multi-threading adds memory pool overhead.\n1351:            // overhead (kernel launches) and height-proportional GPU kernel time\n1405:                    // kernels) is globally visible before we return the DeviceBuffers.\n1424:                            // Sync this thread's stream to ensure all extraction kernels\n1476:    // Note: there are no gpu sync points in this function, so span does not indicate kernel times\n1619:        // interpolation into a single kernel launch.\n1764:        // ── Phase 2: Batch allocate + single kernel launch ──\n1765:        // One big buffer for all interpolated data, one kernel launch for all Case B traces.\n1967:        // Par-Y monomial kernel for high num_y traces","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0