amPerThread` — the proven pattern from Round 0 (task 4, 1.89x) and GKR input eval (task 5, 1.40x) — should yield ~37ms savings.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:### `fold_ple_evals` (`stacked_reduction.rs:689-787`)\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:        2. Launch stacked_reduction_fold_ple() kernel (line 730-739)\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:           → stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel: avg 11μs, 797 instances, 9ms total\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:| `stacked_reduction_fold_ple_kernel` | 9ms | 797 | 11μs | SR PLE fold |\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:Note: `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` (9ms, 797 instances in nsys) is in `logup_zerocheck/utils.cu` — part of the LogUp zerocheck code path, NOT stacked reduction. It is NOT a target of this optimization.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:### Change 2: Multi-stream `fold_ple_evals`\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/stacked_reduction.rs`, function `fold_ple_evals` (lines 689-787)\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/plan.md:   - Each thread launches `stacked_reduction_fold_ple` for its traces, writing to `folded_evals_ptr.add(dst_offset)`\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/report.md:Multi-stream the per-trace sequential kernel loops in Stacked Reduction's `batch_sumcheck_uni_round0_poly` and `fold_ple_evals`. These functions launch ~797 sequential small CUDA kernels on a single stream at APC 300 (~39ms GPU time). The optimization distributes traces across 8 OS threads with per-thread `cudaStreamPerThread` — the same pattern that improved Round 0 constraint eval by 1.89x and GKR input eval by 1.40x — to enable concurrent kernel execution and reduce wall time.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/report.md:- `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/stacked_reduction.rs` — Made `_stacked_reduction_sumcheck_round0` and `_stacked_reduction_fold_ple` FFI functions public for direct use from worker threads\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/report.md:**`fold_ple_evals`**: Pre-computed per-trace `dst_offset` values (the running sum of folded heights × widths). For >= 100 traces per stacked_per_commit group, dispatched traces across 8 threads. Each thread writes to non-overlapping regions of the shared `folded_evals` buffer (no per-thread accumulation needed). Called `current_stream_sync()` after `fill_zero` to ensure visibility to worker streams.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-0140-multistream-stacked-reduction-round0/report.md:4. **fold_ple multi-stream ineffective**: The fold_ple phase contributes only ~9ms GPU time. Multi-streaming would save at most 7ms, but the per-group trace count threshold (>= 100) means only the common_main group activates multi-threading.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md:**Scope**: Only the MLE fold loop (`fold_mle_evals`, rounds 1..n_max). The one-time `fold_ple_evals` (round 0, ~2500 allocs, ~2ms overhead) is NOT changed — it continues to use `DeviceMatrix<EF>` and is handled by a transitional type in the first MLE fold round.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md:To handle the transition from `DeviceMatrix` (output of `fold_ple_evals`) to `ArenaMatrix` (output of MLE fold), use an enum:\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md:**Why**: `fold_ple_evals` (round 0) produces `DeviceMatrix<EF>` via existing `fold_ple_evals_rotate` calls. The first MLE fold round reads these `Owned` matrices and produces `Arena` matrices. From round 2 onward, all matrices are `Arena`. This avoids modifying `fold_ple_evals` entirely.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md:`fold_ple_evals` (unchanged) populates these with `MatrixRef::Owned(DeviceMatrix<EF>)` values. The MLE fold loop transitions them to `MatrixRef::Arena(ArenaMatrix<EF>)` values from round 1 onward.\n/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-14-1200-bulk-alloc-mle-fold-buffers/plan.md:1. **No double-free**: `ArenaMatrix` is `Copy` — it stores a raw pointer but does NOT free memory on drop. Only `FoldArena::drop` frees the big buffers. The `DeviceMatrix` values from `fold_ple_evals` are dropped when they're replaced in the first MLE fold round — their individual `Arc<DeviceBuffer>` handles cleanup normally.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_015KL5x9jcL9JVuaP2tbscZ8","session_id":"b6755d07-23c8-46a3-81f0-36e3b63f2ed6","uuid":"4b04aee2-62b1-402d-84b6-a134c00c1437","timestamp":"2026-04-16T14:57:57.813Z"}
