d previous batch attempts.\n\nFor the specific small AIRs at APC 300, I don't need to handle arbitrary constraints—just the known auto-precompile structures, which simplifies the kernel implementation significantly.\n\nBut the constraint evaluation is still data-driven based on the constraint DAG, so I can't fully specialize at compile time. Let me step back and consider a different angle: pre-computing all kernel launch descriptors during the initialization phase, so each thread in Round 0 just reads pre-built descriptors and launches kernels without the overhead of per-thread setup.\n\nThis would eliminate contention on shared data structures and improve cache locality, though realistically the per-AIR CPU prep is probably only 10-50μs, so even across 78 AIRs per thread, the wall-clock savings might be minimal. The real bottleneck seems to be elsewhere—with 8 threads launching kernels concurrently, memory bandwidth contention when reading trace data is likely the culprit behind the 185ms Round 0 time versus the theoretical 79ms ideal. GPU global memory. If the total memory bandwidth is saturated, each thread's kernels take longer.\n\nGPU memory bandwidth for RTX 4090: ~1 TB/s\nAt APC 300, the trace data is ~811M cells × 4 bytes = ~3.2 GB. Each cell is read once per Round 0 evaluation.\n\nIf all 8 threads are reading concurrently, the effective bandwidth per thread is 1 TB/s / 8 = 125 GB/s. Time to read 3.2 GB / 8 = 400 MB per thread at 125 GB/s = 3.2ms. Plus the constraint evaluation computation.\n\nSo memory bandwidth alone doesn't explain the 185ms. There must be more going on.\n\nLooking at the actual kernel execution times from nsight, I see the zerocheck and logup kernels dominating the workload. The zerocheck kernel runs 539 times totaling 269ms, while logup runs 396 times for 256ms. With 1246 AIRs across both segments but uneven distribution between kernel types, the total GPU time for main Round 0 kernels comes to roughly 600ms. Distributed across 8 thread