rant optimization here. Let me refocus on the WHIR regression itself—there's a 29ms slowdown at APC 300, and I'm wondering if the VPMM page size change from the bulk-page-creation task is responsible. I need to check whether that change was actually kept or reverted.\n\nLooking through the logs, the VPMM modifications appear to have stuck around, and the tune-vpmm-page-size-for-whir task confirmed there's no better configuration—smaller page sizes just hurt GKR more than they help WHIR. So this regression seems baked in unless we fundamentally rethink the memory management strategy, maybe by using a separate VPMM pool for certain allocations.\n\nAnother angle is pre-allocating WHIR's buffers upfront based on the polynomial dimensions, placing them in predictable memory locations before the main VPMM pool gets fragmented by other phases. Looking at the WHIR implementation, each round needs space for the folded polynomial, the RS codeword matrix, and the Merkle tree structures, all of which could be sized and reserved ahead of time.\n\nActually, I found a prior attempt at this—pre-allocating the fold buffers with buffer swapping only yielded about 4ms improvement because it only eliminated 6 VPMM allocations in the early rounds, and each VPMM operation costs around 0.5-1ms. So the 29ms regression must be coming from something else, like the memory layout changes affecting how the Merkle tree hashing behaves or how VPMM pages are being allocated differently.\n\nLooking at the optimization landscape, the most promising targets are Round 0's 43% GPU utilization (suggesting ~100ms of wasted capacity across 8 threads), the GKR inner rounds which are fundamentally sequential at ~326ms, and Trace Commit which is already well-optimized. The Round 0 underutilization is the most interesting puzzle—the nsight profiler shows about 600ms of total GPU kernel time across Round 0, but something's preventing better parallelization.\n\nI'm realizing I need to be more careful about how nsys reports GPU metrics. The total GPU kernel time includes all kernels measured independently, so concurrent kernels on different streams still contribute their full duration to the total. If thread 0 runs kernels for 50ms and thread 1 runs kernels for 40ms concurrently, nsys reports 90ms total GPU time even though the wall clock only sees 50ms of elapsed time due to the overlap.\n\nSo the 600ms total is the sum of all kernel times across all threads. With 8 concurrent streams, if perfectly balanced, I'd expect roughly 600ms/8 = 75ms wall clock per segment. The actual 92.5ms per segment means the utilization is closer to 75/92.5 ≈ 81%, which is much better than my initial 40% estimate. Let me verify this by looking at the actual kernel times for Round 0: the main kernels are taking around 269ms and 256ms, plus some smaller variants totaling roughly 37ms more.\n\nActually, I need to think about this differently. The nsight \"Total Time\" for each kernel is measuring GPU elapsed time from when that kernel starts to when it ends. When multiple kernels run concurrently on different streams, they each measure their own elapsed time independently, but they're competing for the same GPU resources like SMs and memory bandwidth. So if kernels are running in parallel, each one takes longer than it would in isolation because they're sharing those resources.\n\nGiven that the 269ms total for zerocheck kernels is the sum of 539 instances, and each instance is likely inflated by running concurrently with other kernels, the true sequential time would probably be lower—maybe 250-300ms total. That would give me an ideal per-segment time of 31-38ms with 8 threads, but I'm actually seeing 92.5ms per segment. So I'm getting only 33-41% utilization, which suggests the bottleneck is memory bandwidth contention and SM sharing when running 8 concurrent instances.\n\nThis makes sense—the kernels are memory-bandwidth-bound, and running 8 threads concurrently saturates the memory bandwidth,