ion processes 623 traces sequentially on the default stream, so distributing them could help. But I need to check if `fold_ple_evals` is even part of the \"STARK excl trace\" timing span. Looking at the code flow, `fold_ple_evals` is called once per proof after Round 0, followed by `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` for per-MLE-round evaluation.\n\nCounting the operations per trace, I'm seeing 623 `fold_selectors_round0` calls with varying kernel execution times depending on trace size. The total time is probably dominated by larger traces, putting us in the 20-50ms range. Multi-streaming could save a meaningful chunk of that.\n\nThe real question is whether the overhead is worth it—we're looking at maybe a 2-3% improvement on the overall STARK time, which is measurable but not huge. I should also check if VPMM gets unhappy with the temporary allocation patterns from multi-streaming, since that's been a pain point before.\n\nThere's also the bandwidth question: eight threads hammering the trace data simultaneously could saturate memory, especially on larger traces. And I'd need to be careful with thread safety when writing results back to the Vec—pre-allocating and filling indexed positions instead of collecting iteratively.\n\nActually, there's another angle: deduplicating the selector fold by `n` could cut kernel launches from 623 down to ~20, which combined with multi-streaming the trace fold might compound the gains. But honestly, the expected impact is still pretty modest.\n\nLet me step back and think about what could actually move the needle more significantly.\n\nIf multi-streaming `fold_ple_evals` saved 30ms, that's only 2.6% improvement—but at this stage of optimization, even 2-3% is worth pursuing. The catch is that the fold outputs feed into every MLE round, so I'd need to synchronize all streams before those rounds kick off.\n\nLooking at the feasible options, multi-streaming the fold has medium probability and moderate complexity for 15-35ms of savings, deduplicating selectors is simple and high-probability for 5-10ms, and pre-computing static Round 0 Phase 1 items at keygen is medium probability for 5-15ms.\n\nActually, I can combine the first two approaches—multi-stream the fold processing across 8 OS threads while deduplicating selector construction by unique `n` values, then share the folded results across traces with the same `n` via Arc. This should cut the fold time from roughly 30-50ms down to 15-25ms, saving 15-30ms overall on the STARK trace generation, plus another 3-5ms from the selector deduplication itself.\n\nThe total savings lands around 18-35ms, which is borderline measurable but a clean, low-risk win. Though reconsidering the actual bottleneck: at APC 300, processing all 623 traces touches about 3.2 GB of stacked matrix data, which at typical memory bandwidth takes only 3-5ms to read. The remaining 20-40ms of fold overhead is likely kernel launches, allocations, and CPU coordination, so multi-threading should help there.\n\nLet me check the nsight profiler data to pin down where the time actually goes. I'm seeing the fold kernels account for around 11ms total across nearly a thousand instances, but I need to verify whether these are from the stacked reduction path or the actual MLE fold operation.\n\nIf those kernels are indeed from `fold_ple_evals_rotate`, then the GPU work is just 11ms. Adding in allocation overhead for nearly a thousand allocations and CPU loop costs, the whole operation probably takes 15-20ms wall clock. Multi-streaming could cut the GPU portion down to 3ms by overlapping work across threads, saving maybe 5-10ms overall.\n\nEven with selector dedup shaving off another 3-5ms, I'm looking at a best-case scenario of 8-15ms total—right at or below the 10ms success threshold, which feels marginal.\n\nI need to figure out whether those 797 `fold_ple_from_evals_kernel` instances are coming from `fold_ple_evals` or the stacked reduction, since both use that kernel. At this point, though, 