stribution overhead would eat into those gains.\n\nRealistically, multi-streaming might save 8-12ms—not a huge win. But I just realized something important looking at the actual fold implementation: the code is allocating a device buffer for each trace and then calling the fold kernel with unsafe pointer operations. This is where the real bottleneck might be hiding.\n\nWith 623 traces at APC 300, we're doing 623 separate allocations and 623 kernel launches. But if I group traces by their unique heights, I'd only have around 20-25 distinct groups. I could compute the fold once per group and then have each trace just clone an Arc pointer to the shared result—that's 623 cheap Arc clones instead of 623 expensive allocations and kernel launches. The savings would be substantial: roughly 600 fewer VPMM allocations, which translates to 2-3ms just from avoiding allocation overhead, plus another few milliseconds from reducing kernel launch overhead.\n\nThe same logic applies to the selector base construction—instead of 623 host-to-device uploads, I'd do just 25. Combined, this could save 8-10ms, though that's still on the marginal side.\n\nLooking at all my options, I need to pick the one with the best return on effort. Multi-stream fold evaluation with selector deduplication looks like it could give 15-25ms of improvement with moderate complexity, while pre-computing static items at keygen time would only save 5-15ms. The first option has better expected impact relative to the work involved.\n\nBut wait—I'm probably tunnel-visioning on individual components. What if there's dead time between phases that I'm not accounting for? Between Round 0 finishing and the MLE rounds starting, there's polynomial coefficient generation, then folding the PLE evaluations using the Round 0 challenge. That transition might be where I can find bigger wins.\n\nNow looking at what happens between GKR and Round 0—there's the fractional sumcheck proof, challenge squeezing, and then the CPU prep phase that takes 20-40ms. The question is whether any of this can overlap with GKR itself, since steps 2-4 are running sequentially on CPU anyway.\n\nDuring the ~250ms GKR fractional sumcheck, the CPU sits idle waiting for GPU results. I can identify which Round 0 Phase 1 computations don't depend on `xi` (which isn't available until after GKR): building `sels_per_trace_base`, extracting constraint degree tables, pre-allocating the batch array, computing buffer sizes, and sorting work items. These could all run in parallel while GKR is still executing on the GPU.\n\nHowever, there's a complication — `sels_per_trace_base` construction involves host-to-device uploads, which would contend with the GPU's fractional sumcheck kernels. I need to check whether those uploads would actually block or if there's enough GPU bandwidth to handle both simultaneously.\n\nActually, looking at the upload sizes (96-3K bytes per trace), they're tiny and go through the async copy engine rather than the compute pipeline, so they shouldn't interfere. My plan is to parallelize Phase 1 by running a background CPU thread during GKR that pre-computes everything except the xi and lambda-dependent items — the selector base construction, extract tables, buffer sizes, and work item sorting. Once GKR finishes and those values are squeezed, I only need to compute the remaining xi-dependent pieces before building the final work item.\n\nThis could cut Phase 1 from 20-40ms down to 5-10ms. The tricky part is that the background thread needs access to the segment's proving context, which is available in `prove_zerocheck_and_logup_gpu`. I need to find where in the GKR fractional sumcheck code I can spawn this thread.\n\nLooking at the flow, I can spawn the background thread right before `fractional_sumcheck_gpu()` runs and join it after GKR completes but before xi and lambda sampling. This overlaps the pre-computation work with the ~250ms GKR fractional sumcheck. The xi-independent work—allocating selector buffers, making FFI calls for buffer sizes, and extracting tables—should take around 10-20ms total, so there's real savings here. unique degrees (~1-3ms)\n- Sort: ~0.5ms\n- d_batch_array allocation: ~0.5ms\nTotal xi-independent: ~7-14ms\n\nFrom Phase 1 total of ~20-40ms, saving 7-14ms reduces it to ~13-26ms.\n\nBut we also overlap this with GKR (~250ms), so the effective saving is the full xi-independent time (~7-14ms) since it's fully hidden behind GKR.\n\nWait actually, we'd be moving ~7-14ms of CPU work into the GKR period, which is ~250ms of GPU work. The CPU is mostly idle during GKR (just handling per-round D2H and transcript operations). So the overlap is free.\n\nThe net saving on STARK excl trace: ~7-14ms. This is real wall-clock savings because Phase 1 is on the critical path (after GKR, before Round 0 kernels).\n\nThough 7-14ms is marginal, let me see what else could be precomputed. The `lambda_combinations` computation launches GPU kernels per AIR that can't run during GKR since the GPU is busy. If I move it after GKR and overlap with xi-dependent Phase 1 work, things get messy.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: precompute all xi-independent Round 0 Phase 1 items at keygen time instead of during GKR. At keygen we already know the AIR properties, proving key structure, and buffer sizes for all AIRs.\n\nWhat we don't know yet is which traces appear in each segment, plus the xi and lambda challenges. But we can still precompute buffer sizes, extract tables, and work item templates at keygen time, then at proving time just filter for the segment's traces, compute xi and lambda dependent items, and fill in device pointers. This saves the buffer size FFI calls and extract table computation from every segment—roughly 5-10ms per proof.\n\nIt's a clean approach but the savings are modest. Let me look at the `lambda_combinations` computation more closely.\n\nThe code iterates over all AIRs in the proving key rather than just the current segment's traces. With 623