ffectively disappears from the critical path.\n\nThe net savings on STARK excluding trace is around 10-20 milliseconds with minimal risk—just CPU work and small uploads on a background thread that won't interfere with GPU kernels. But that's below the project's typical threshold, so I'm thinking about combining this with other optimizations: deduplicating sels_per_trace_base and fold_selectors_round0 by their `n` values, plus using multi-stream fold_ple_evals to squeeze out another 3-5 milliseconds each, which could push the total savings to 25-40 milliseconds.\n\nThe tradeoff is that bundling four changes together makes debugging harder if something breaks. I'm leaning toward focusing on the highest-impact single change—multi-streaming fold_ple_evals with selector deduplication and the GKR overlap—but now I'm wondering if I'm missing something by not looking at the full picture. Let me examine the NSYS profile more carefully to spot any gaps I haven't considered.\n\nActually, I'm noticing the mat_evals fold in fold_ple_evals_rotate processes all traces sequentially, and each one goes through the same steps: folding the preprocessed trace if it exists, folding each cached main, and folding common. There might be parallelization potential here I haven't fully explored.\n\nWith roughly 963 kernel launches across 623 traces running one after another, applying the same multi-streaming pattern that worked in Round 0 could help—distributing traces across 8 threads where each thread handles its own stream. That could cut the fold_ple_evals time from 15-20ms down to 5-8ms, saving around 10-15ms. Combined with selector deduplication saving another 3-5ms, I'm looking at a total of 13-20ms improvement, though that's still on the borderline of what's worth the added complexity.\n\nBut wait—I'm realizing `fold_ple_evals` was never actually targeted for multi-streaming optimization, even though earlier phases like Round 0 and GKR input eval saw real wins from it. It's still doing